# LLMs for Audio Description Based Evaluation of Content Severity in Films

## Abstract
Technological advances such as the internet have caused an explosive of access to an enormous volume of movies from all around the globe. Automatically identifying different types and intensity levels of sensitive content is important to ensure the safe experiences of users of all ages. We extract representations of 550 English language films from subtitles and audio descriptions using pretrained LLMs such as RoBERTa to classify the document level ordinal severity of content across six different sensitivity categories: themes, violence, language, drug use, nudity and sex. We experiment with pooling and document partitioning strategies to provide the best document-level classification when SVMs and Logistic Regression is applied to the context embeddings. Adding audio descriptions to subtitles improved absolute macro F1 by 1.9-5.4 across the categories, demonstrating that most of the textual markers of sensitive content are included in subtitles and audio descriptions are a more computationally intensive dataset than they are worth for document-level classification. However, by attempting weak localisation of sensitive content we saw significant changes in rates of sensitive content detection by including audio descriptions. RoBERTa models pretrained on offensive tweet detection achieved state of the art classification results in four categories, affirming the similarity of social media content moderation to film classification, enabling further redeployment of models and dataset.


## Data
The post-processed data is available on Kaggle [here](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/nytes1/subtitles-and-ads-of-550-films-with-acb-ratings).

### Australian Classification Board (ACB) Ratings
A 7 point ordinal rating of severity across six content categories: themes, violence, language, drug use, nudity and sex provided by the Australian government for films since 2013. The tools required to scrape this information is included in this repository. 

### AudioVault Audio Descriptions
AudioVault is a community driven organisation, with significant resource limitations, which provides MP3 files from films which include additional narration for visually impaired people (known as audio descriptions). Speech-to-text (STT) models were required to prepare the data for LLM usage. We can make the original audio files available to reduce burden on AV.

### OpenSubtitle Text Files
Crowdsourced subtitles were used for the films to asess the quality of audio description transcriptions and their additional predictive power, compared to dialogue.  

